Yeah—this gives us a good two-act Cage structure: first he has to learn that Yu-Gi-Oh logic is real, then the “exit” dumps him somewhere where bluffing with card-game logic absolutely should not work—but Nicolas Cage tries it anyway.

Nicolas Cage woke up face-down on a stone platform.

This, by itself, was not particularly alarming.

There had been films.

He raised his head.

Across from him stood a teenager with impossible hair.

Not merely unusual hair.

Impossible hair.

Black spikes. Blond bangs. Red edges.

The architectural confidence of a Gothic cathedral.

Cage stared at him.

The teenager stared back.

Then a mechanical device unfolded from his arm.

Five cards appeared in his hand.

Cage slowly looked down.

There was an identical machine strapped to his own forearm.

He closed his eyes.

“No.”

A computerized voice announced:

DUEL BEGIN.

Cage opened his eyes.

“No.”

The teenager pointed dramatically.

“I summon Celtic Guardian!”

A glowing warrior erupted onto the platform.

Nicolas Cage stepped backward.

“Oh, come on.”

The warrior raised its sword.

Cage inspected his own hand.

Five cards.

Mystical Space Typhoon.

Pot of Greed.

Kuriboh.

Castle of Dark Illusions.

And—

Cage stopped.

He held up the final card.

It depicted Nicolas Cage.

The title read:

NICOLAS CAGE, RELUCTANT TREASURE HUNTER

ATK: 1800
DEF: 1600

Effect:

When summoned, this monster may inspect one suspicious object on the field.

Cage stared at it.

Then at himself.

Then back at the card.

“That is insulting.”

His opponent shouted, “Your move!”

“Give me a second! I’m processing something existential!”

A timer appeared.

27

“Apparently I don’t have a second.”

Cage slapped Pot of Greed onto the machine.

“I PLAY POT OF GREED!”

The machine activated.

Cage paused.

He looked around.

Nobody reacted.

He frowned.

“I feel like someone should have explained what that does.”

The computerized voice said:

DRAW TWO CARDS.

“Oh.”

He drew two cards.

“That’s actually excellent.”

The duel continued.

At first Cage treated it like combat.

This went badly.

Then he treated it like poker.

This went slightly better.

Then he realized something.

Every time either duelist did anything remotely important, they announced it first.

Not occasionally.

Always.

“I ACTIVATE MY TRAP CARD!”

“I SUMMON DARK MAGICIAN!”

“I SACRIFICE TWO MONSTERS!”

“I END MY TURN!”

Cage slowly lowered his cards.

Nobody here could keep a secret.

He smiled.

“Oh.”

His opponent hesitated.

“What?”

“You people narrate your strategy.”

“It’s a duel.”

“You narrate your strategy out loud.”

“That’s how dueling works.”

Cage began laughing.

His opponent took a step backward.

“What?”

“This entire civilization is vulnerable to people who understand lying.”

“You can’t lie in a duel!”

Cage stopped laughing.

“Why?”

The teenager opened his mouth.

Nothing came out.

Cage leaned forward.

“Why?”

“Because…”

“Yes?”

“The rules.”

“Where are the rules?”

The teenager pointed vaguely upward.

Cage looked up.

There was nothing there.

“Oh, this is magnificent.”

He drew a card.

“I place one card face-down.”

His opponent narrowed his eyes.

“A trap.”

“Maybe.”

“What is it?”

Cage smiled.

“You’ll find out.”

The teenager seemed genuinely disturbed.

Three turns later, Cage discovered the deeper problem.

He was losing badly.

His opponent had summoned a massive dragon.

Cage had a Kuriboh.

The dragon had 3000 attack points.

Kuriboh looked like a frightened pom-pom.

Cage looked at Kuriboh.

Kuriboh looked at Cage.

“We are badly outgunned.”

Kuriboh squeaked.

The teenager raised his hand.

“Blue-Eyes White Dragon! Attack!”

The dragon opened its jaws.

Cage studied the arena.

Stone platform.

Floating score displays.

Holograms.

Duel disks.

Audience.

Audience.

Cage stopped.

There were hundreds of spectators surrounding them.

But they were repeating.

Same faces.

Same gestures.

Same three cheers.

He looked farther.

The horizon repeated too.

Stone columns.

Cloud.

Tower.

Stone columns.

Cloud.

Tower.

Cage whispered:

“This isn’t the world.”

The dragon roared.

Cage ignored it.

“This is an arena.”

“Attack!”

“This is a game.”

The dragon fired.

Cage threw himself sideways.

His life points dropped to 400.

Cage rolled onto his back.

“Very immersive game.”

He looked at his duel disk.

There was a tiny symbol near the hinge.

A gear.

Settings.

Cage stared at it.

Then at his opponent.

“Have you ever pressed this?”

“Pressed what?”

Cage pressed the gear.

Everything froze.

The dragon stopped mid-roar.

The crowd froze.

His opponent froze with one finger dramatically extended.

A menu appeared.

PAUSE

DECK

SETTINGS

CONCEDE

EXIT TUTORIAL

Cage stared.

“Tutorial?”

He turned toward his frozen opponent.

“You’ve been talking to me like Pharaoh Seto Sun Tzu for forty minutes.”

He selected:

EXIT TUTORIAL.

The world vanished.

—

Nicolas Cage hit dirt.

Hard.

Again.

He sat up.

No holograms.

No duel disk.

No impossible-haired teenager.

Instead there were horses.

Soldiers.

Standards snapping in cold air.

Stone walls rose in the distance.

Men in armor marched along a road.

Cage looked around.

“Oh, good.”

A mounted soldier approached.

He shouted something in Spanish.

Cage understood just enough Spanish to become concerned.

“What year is it?”

The soldier stared.

Cage tried again.

“¿Año?”

The soldier answered.

Cage went silent.

He looked toward the enormous fortified city ahead.

Granada.

He closed his eyes.

“No.”

A man beside him said, “You are with the Castilian forces?”

Cage opened his eyes.

The man spoke accented English.

Cage pointed toward Granada.

“Is that Granada?”

“Yes.”

“Granada Granada?”

The man stared.

“Yes.”

“As in last major stronghold of the Nasrid kingdom Granada?”

The man looked increasingly suspicious.

Cage whispered:

“I escaped Yu-Gi-Oh into the Reconquista.”

The man crossed himself.

“That sentence contains several things I do not understand.”

“Neither do I.”

Cage checked his pockets.

Wallet.

Car keys.

Beef jerky.

And one Yu-Gi-Oh card.

He slowly removed it.

NICOLAS CAGE, RELUCTANT TREASURE HUNTER

Cage stared at it.

“Oh, absolutely not.”

He put it back.

The English-speaking soldier pointed toward the camp.

“You should speak with the commanders.”

“No.”

“You appear to be some kind of foreign nobleman.”

“No.”

“Your clothing is extremely strange.”

“That part is fair.”

Cage suddenly became aware that he was wearing a black leather jacket in fifteenth-century Spain.

Within twenty minutes, through a sequence of misunderstandings too bureaucratic to stop, Nicolas Cage found himself inside a command tent.

Several military officers were arguing over maps.

Cage stood silently in the corner.

He had decided his safest strategy was to contribute nothing to history.

This lasted approximately twelve seconds.

One commander moved a wooden marker.

Cage frowned.

He tried not to.

Another marker moved.

Cage frowned harder.

One of the commanders noticed.

“You disagree?”

Cage shook his head.

“No.”

“You made a face.”

“I have a very expressive face.”

“Why?”

“That position looks exposed.”

Silence.

Cage immediately regretted speaking.

The commander pointed to the map.

“Explain.”

“I really shouldn’t.”

“Explain.”

Cage stared at the map.

Then something clicked.

Rows.

Zones.

Units.

Terrain.

Resources.

He had just spent forty minutes learning a ridiculous combat system based around positioning, sacrifice, sequencing, and forcing your opponent to reveal intentions.

Cage leaned toward the map.

“Oh no.”

“What?”

“I recognize the interface.”

Nobody knew what that meant.

Cage picked up two wooden markers.

“If that force moves here, they have to answer.”

He moved another.

“If they answer there, this route becomes weak.”

One commander nodded slowly.

Cage continued.

“You’re not attacking the piece.”

He pointed.

“You’re attacking the response.”

The tent became quiet.

Cage suddenly realized he had an audience.

He dropped the marker.

“That is all I am saying.”

A commander asked, “Where did you learn warfare?”

Cage considered several answers.

Finally:

“Children’s card games.”

Nobody laughed.

Cage looked around.

“That was not a joke.”

Over the following days, Cage remained determined not to alter history.

Unfortunately, people kept asking his opinion.

And because Nicolas Cage possessed the extremely dangerous combination of partial knowledge and total confidence, he kept answering.

He compared cavalry reserves to trap cards.

He described supply routes as “continuous spell effects.”

He called fortified positions “high-defense monsters.”

At one point he referred to artillery as “direct life-point damage.”

The translators simply stopped translating that part.

Yet underneath the nonsense, some of his observations were useful.

Mostly because Cage had accidentally discovered something obvious:

games, battles, negotiations, and treasure hunts all shared one feature.

People acted based on what they believed the other side would do next.

And that meant expectations could be manipulated.

One evening, Cage stood outside the camp watching the Alhambra glow against the mountains.

The English-speaking soldier joined him.

“You seem troubled.”

Cage nodded.

“I know what happens.”

“To Granada?”

“Yes.”

“How?”

Cage looked at him.

“That is complicated.”

“Are you a prophet?”

“No.”

“A sorcerer?”

“No.”

“Then what are you?”

Cage thought about this.

“An actor.”

The soldier frowned.

“You pretend to be other men?”

“Yes.”

“For money?”

“Yes.”

The soldier considered that.

“You are a professional liar.”

Cage looked slightly offended.

Then slightly impressed.

“That is an unexpectedly efficient description.”

The soldier pointed toward the card sticking out of Cage’s pocket.

“What is that?”

Cage removed it.

His own illustrated face stared back.

“This apparently.”

The soldier examined it.

“Is this a religious icon?”

“No.”

“A royal seal?”

“No.”

“A playing card?”

“Unfortunately, yes.”

The soldier handed it back.

“You carry a playing card with your own face on it?”

Cage looked toward Granada.

“You know, when you say it like that, I sound insane.”

“Yes.”

“Thank you.”

Eventually the capitulation came.

Cage stayed away from the important rooms.

No speeches.

No interventions.

No heroic charge.

History had enough people inside it already.

Instead, he watched.

Standards changed.

Soldiers moved.

People celebrated.

Other people mourned.

And Cage noticed something the card game had not taught him.

Winning the board and understanding what the board meant were not the same thing.

He stood silently for a while.

Then the Yu-Gi-Oh card in his pocket began glowing.

Cage sighed.

“Of course.”

He pulled it out.

The text had changed.

NICOLAS CAGE, RELUCTANT TREASURE HUNTER

New effect:

Once per journey, when the player recognizes that victory and meaning are not equivalent, return this card to its owner’s hand.

Cage stared at it.

“That is suspiciously specific.”

The card flashed.

—

Cage landed on carpeting.

Barnes & Noble.

Again.

Same bookstore.

Same employee.

The employee looked up from behind the counter.

Nicolas Cage was covered in dirt and carrying a piece of fifteenth-century bread.

The employee stared.

Cage stared back.

Neither spoke.

Finally the employee said:

“Biography section?”

Cage shook his head.

“History.”

“Second floor.”

Cage started walking.

Then stopped.

“Do you have Yu-Gi-Oh cards?”

“Near the registers.”

Cage turned very slowly.

A display rack stood beside him.

Booster packs.

Starter decks.

Collector tins.

Cage approached.

He picked up one pack.

On the front was Blue-Eyes White Dragon.

Cage narrowed his eyes.

The cashier said, “Those are five ninety-nine.”

Cage placed the pack back.

“No.”

He walked toward the history section.

After three steps he returned.

Picked up the pack.

Placed six dollars on the counter.

“Actually, I need to check something.”

Outside, sitting alone on a bench, Nicolas Cage opened the booster pack.

Five ordinary cards.

Then the sixth.

Cage froze.

The card depicted a medieval battlefield.

In the far corner, almost impossible to notice, was a tiny man in a black leather jacket moving wooden markers across a map.

The title read:

MYSTERIOUS TACTICIAN OF GRANADA

Cage stared at it for almost a minute.

Then looked directly ahead.

“No more tutorials.”

The piece I like most in that one is the bridge: Cage doesn’t become some master general because he played Yu-Gi-Oh. He recognizes that games abstract real operators—sequencing, forcing responses, incomplete information, sacrifice, positioning—and then discovers the abstraction becomes much more morally complicated when the “pieces” are actual people. That gives the ridiculous premise just enough backbone to carry it.